Summary of important user-visible changes for datatypes 1.3.1:
--------------------------------------------------------------

 This release fixes `keyHash` and `keyMatch`, and the `missing` class along
 with how it interoperates with `datetime`, `duration` and
 `calendarDuration`.

 `keyHash` and `keyMatch` were wrong in ways that made distinct values
 collide, so **hash codes change** and stored codes from 1.3.0 and earlier
 are invalid and must be recomputed.  What changes and what does not is set
 out in full below.

 The `missing` work is a set of parity corrections measured against MATLAB
 R2024a.  A missing value now converts, compares and concatenates as MATLAB
 defines, and may be concatenated with or assigned into a `datetime`,
 `duration` or `calendarDuration` array from either side.  None of it breaks
 anything that previously worked: every one of these replaces a wrong answer
 or an outright error.

 ** `keyMatch` no longer decides identity by comparing hash codes.  Hash
    equality is not key equality, and any collision reported two distinct
    keys as one.  It now compares the values themselves, with missing
    values comparing equal to one another, after the existing class and
    size checks -- so `1` and `int8 (1)` remain different keys, as do a row
    and a column holding the same elements.  `keyHash` agrees with it: two
    values that `keyMatch` reports as the same key always have the same
    hash code.  The converse does not hold, since distinct keys are
    permitted to share a hash code.

 ** `keyHash` hashed only the FIRST CHARACTER of a character vector.  It
    passed the array as an N-by-1 char matrix, which was read by taking the
    first row and discarding the rest.  Every character vector was
    therefore keyed on its first character alone, so `keyHash ('hello')`
    and `keyHash ('hxxxx')` were equal and `keyMatch` called them the same
    key.  Character arrays are now hashed whole, in column-major order.

 ** Elements of a cell array of character vectors and of a `string` array
    were concatenated before hashing, which lost the boundaries between
    them: `{'ab', 'c'}` and `{'a', 'bc'}` were one key, and `keyMatch`
    agreed.  Elements are now hashed with their lengths, so no two
    different splittings of the same characters can collide.  `keyHash ({})`
    also no longer errors, and stays distinct from `keyHash ({''})`.

 ** Values that compare equal but differ in memory are now one key.  A
    negative zero keys as `+0`, and every `NaN` keys alike whatever
    produced it.  The second half is not academic: on x86-64 the literal
    `NaN` is positive while `0/0` and `Inf - Inf` are negative, so
    `seconds (NaN)` and `seconds (0/0)` were different `duration` keys.

 ** A zoned `datetime` is keyed on the instant rather than on its wall
    clock and zone name, so the same moment named in two zones is one key.
    An unzoned array, a zoned array and a leap-second array remain three
    separate frames and are never equal to one another, and the two
    instants that share a wall clock across a fall-back stay distinct.

 ** `categorical` is keyed on its element labels, plus the category
    ordering for an ordinal array alone.  The `Protected` flag is no longer
    part of the key, and neither is a non-ordinal array's category list --
    so two non-ordinal arrays carrying the same labels are now the same
    key however their categories differ or are ordered, matching MATLAB.
    Ordinal arrays are unaffected: ordinality and the full category
    ordering remain part of identity.

 ** `keyMatch` raises for a value that cannot be a key.  It asks `keyHash`
    whether each input is hashable before comparing anything, so a `table`, a
    `struct`, a cell array that is not a cell array of character vectors, and
    a function handle are each rejected by name.  Answering would assert that
    two values are the same key, or are different keys, when no key exists
    for either.  MATLAB rejects `table` in the same way and for the same
    reason; it does accept `struct`, `cell` and `function_handle` as keys,
    which this package does not hash yet, and they will be accepted here as
    soon as it does.

 ** `keyHash` no longer ignores a zero offset basis.  The optional `base`
    argument was tested for truth rather than for presence, so
    `keyHash (X, uint64 (0))` silently used the default basis instead of
    zero, and an invalid base that happened to be falsy -- `0`, `false`, or a
    `uint64` array containing a zero -- skipped validation altogether while
    `1` was correctly rejected.  A zero basis is now honoured and every
    invalid base raises.  An empty `base` still means no base, as before.

 ** A subclass of `datetime`, `duration`, `calendarDuration`, `categorical` or
    `string` no longer shares its parent's key.  The `keyHash` methods wrote
    the declared class name into the hash rather than the actual one, so an
    instance of a derived class hashed identically to a base instance holding
    the same data, and `keyMatch` called them the same key.  Hash codes for
    the base classes themselves are unaffected.

 ** `keyMatch` is faster on the classdef types.  Having both hash codes in
    hand from the check above, it uses them to reject: codes that differ
    prove the keys differ, which skips a deep comparison costing an order of
    magnitude more.  Comparing two 200,000-element `string` arrays that differ
    in one element is about 13 times faster than before, and `categorical`
    about 5 times.  The codes are never used to accept, since distinct keys
    are permitted to share one.

 ** Which hash codes change, precisely.  Measured input by input against
    1.3.0:

      changed:   every character vector of two or more characters, and
                 every character matrix; every cell array of character
                 vectors; every `string`; every `categorical`; every
                 `datetime`; and any numeric value whose data actually
                 contains a negative zero or a sign-set `NaN`.

      unchanged: all other numeric and logical values, of every class,
                 including `0` and the literal `NaN` themselves; the empty
                 character vector and single characters; every `duration`
                 except one holding a `NaN` produced by arithmetic; and
                 every `calendarDuration`.

    `calendarDuration` needed no change: `calmonths (12)` and `calyears (1)`
    were already one key, and `calmonths (1)` and `caldays (30)` already
    were not.

 ** Hash codes remain identical across workers and Octave sessions, which
    is what this package offers over MATLAB's randomly seeded `keyHash`.
    They are not stable across this version boundary.

 ** `missing` no longer converts to text.  `cellstr (missing)` returned
    `{'<missing>'}`, and concatenating a missing value with a cell array of
    character vectors filled it with `''`.  Both now raise.  A missing value
    has no text, so answering asserted a conversion that does not exist;
    MATLAB refuses both in the same way.  `dispstrings` is unaffected and
    still yields `<missing>`, which is a display placeholder rather than a
    conversion.

 ** `isequal` and `isequaln` on a missing value answer instead of raising.
    Both accepted only `missing` and `categorical` operands and raised
    `unsupported input types` for anything else, so `isequal (missing, NaN)`
    was an error rather than `false`.  `isequal` is now false whenever a
    missing value takes part, exactly as it is for `NaN`, and `isequaln` is
    true when every operand is entirely missing and all shapes agree.  Each
    operand answers for itself through its own `ismissing`, so `datetime`,
    `duration`, `calendarDuration`, `categorical` and `string` are all
    handled, and the floating types count `NaN` as missing.

 ** Concatenating a missing value with a `duration` no longer exhausts the
    recursion limit.  `cat` recognised `duration` as the result type but had
    no branch to build one, so the missing value survived unconverted and the
    call dispatched back into itself: `[missing, duration(1, 0, 0)]` failed
    with `max_recursion_depth exceeded`.  The result takes its display format
    from the real operand rather than from the all-NaN filler, so
    `[missing, hours(1)]` renders as `NaN hr` and `1 hr` instead of imposing
    the default `hh:mm:ss`.

 ** `duration (missing)` no longer raises.  It built its input as
    `NaN (size (M))`, a single-column matrix, where `duration` reads a
    numeric argument as an N-by-3 `[h m s]` matrix, so every call failed with
    `X must have 3 columns`.  All six conversions -- `double`, `single`,
    `categorical`, `datetime`, `duration` and `calendarDuration` -- now
    return an array shaped like the missing array they were given.

 ** A missing value may now be concatenated with a `datetime`, `duration` or
    `calendarDuration` array from either side.  It worked only on the left,
    where `missing` converts it; on the right each class handled the call
    itself and had no branch for the type, so `[duration(1, 0, 0), missing]`
    raised `cat: duration/horzcat method failed` and the direct `cat` form
    raised the rawer `duration: invalid input to constructor`.  Both operand
    orders, both orientations and `cat` along any dimension now work, and the
    missing element reads back as that class's own marker -- `NaT` for
    `datetime`, `NaN` for the other two.

 ** A missing value may now be assigned into a `datetime` or
    `calendarDuration` array.  Both refused it while `duration` accepted it,
    so the three classes disagreed with one another.  `D(3) = missing` now
    assigns `NaT` or `NaN`, in the array's own time zone where it has one.  A
    gap opened by growing past the end is unchanged and still follows each
    class: `NaT` for `datetime`, zero for `duration` and `calendarDuration`.

 ** Concatenating a missing value with a zoned or custom-format `datetime` no
    longer loses that state.  The filler was built as a plain `NaT`, so a
    zoned operand failed outright -- an unzoned value cannot sit in a zoned
    array -- and a non-default format was silently replaced by the default:
    `[missing, datetime(2020, 1, 1, 'Format', 'yyyy')]` displayed
    `01-Jan-2020` rather than `2020`.  The filler now carries both the time
    zone and the format of the real operand.
